zl1 -- the SECOND heat cause (cpufreq governor), THIRD device run, 2026-09-25
============================================================================

One boot throughout: boot_id 2fbf9f8e-deea-4955-ad0c-6bddf0fb14f5 -- the same boot as docs 168, 170 and
172. Read again AFTER this run: unchanged, four cores `interactive`, `systemctl is-system-running` =
running, 0 failed units. Nothing was flashed, no partition was written, no block device was opened, no
service was restarted, the container was never stopped.

THIS IS THE FIRST RUN OF THE INSTRUMENT AS DOCS 172 LEFT IT, and the first run of the pre-hold on a real
phone. Its verdict is NO RETURN: the instrument REFUSED to print a price, and printed the DIRECTION of
the difference that stopped it (4.1 C BELOW window A, on thermal_zone9). The second run printed
`COST-MEASURED 3.5 C` on the same phone in the same shape; this one does not.

Every number below is the device's own. THE DEVICE CLOCK IS WRONG (it reads 1970-02-13) -- the real dates
and times are the host ones in this header -- but it ADVANCES at the normal rate, so the `/proc/uptime`
figures the instrument prints are the honest clock for everything in section B, and they are what the
timeline in section C is computed from.

Host: this laptop (launched 2026-09-25T22:43:45Z).  Device: root@10.15.19.82 (rndis0), serial 33e80afe.
The instrument was pushed with `scp ... /tmp/` and its sha256 on the device is the same as the shippped
file's: 5389fffeaa8709e48ad1c09aa3f93a27eb80fc23704bff2865cc546fa5d5451a.

The only thing this experiment writes is four cpufreq files, and the value it writes back is the fix's own
(`interactive`). This run was started with `--keep`, so it NAMED its scratch directory instead of removing
it (/tmp/zl1-governor-temp-ab.ubXCQ9, holding pre.1, pre.2, win.A, win.B, back and deltas) and those files
were pulled to this laptop before anything else touched the device.

