  now:        interactive cpu0=interactive cpu1=interactive cpu2=interactive cpu3=interactive

== the refusals, on their own terms (nothing is written until all of them have passed)
   A. panic guard: all 1 download_mode parameter(s) read 0 -- a panic reboots, not EDL
   B. instrument: readable (/tmp/zl1-thermal.sh)
   C. cores:      all 4 are writable and offer both 'interactive' and 'performance'
   D. as installed: all 4 core(s) read 'interactive' -- the fix is in place (this is window A)
   C. write path: wrote 'interactive' back to all 4 core(s) and read 'interactive'
                  back -- writable in fact, and every core agrees on what it read.

== the pre-hold -- is this phone holding still, before a window is read at all?
   Two readings 10s apart must agree on EVERY tsens zone to within 0.5 C before window A is
   read. Up to 300s. A phone that never holds still cannot be priced, and this is refused
   before the intervention: this is the one exit from this script that writes nothing at all.
   IT IS HOLDING STILL: after 20s, the largest change on any tsens zone in the last 10s
   was 0.4 C (signed 0.4 C, on thermal_zone16 (tsens_tz_sensor15)). Window A
   below is a STATE, not a point on a curve -- which is what makes B and C comparable to it.

== window A -- as installed (every core on 'interactive', nothing written yet)
   busy 0.82 of 4 cores; 38 zone(s) read
   wrote 'performance' to all 4 core(s) and read 'performance' back: the cores are PINNED from
   here until the undo (this is the image's own state -- the second heat cause put back).

== window B -- the cores PINNED (the one change this experiment makes)
   busy 0.54 of 4 cores
   wrote 'interactive' to all 4 core(s) and read 'interactive' back: this is the state it started in.

== the wait -- does window A's reading come back before the control window reads it?
   window A's hottest tsens zone: 47.0 C. Waiting up to 240s (every 10s) for EVERY
   tsens zone to come back to within 0.5 C of its OWN window A reading before window C reads
   anything. Every zone, and not just the hottest one: the hottest zone CHANGES HANDS while a run goes
   on, so a test that follows it compares one zone's number with another's and calls it a return
   (docs 172 -- the second device run passed that test while being 4.2 C down).
   IT DID NOT COME BACK within 240s: the largest difference from window A is 4.1
   BELOW window A's reading of the SAME zone (-4.1 C, on thermal_zone9 (tsens_tz_sensor8)). Two things can look like this and this instrument cannot
   tell them apart: the intervention's warming has not decayed (thermal mass), or the phone moved on
   its own by more than the margin while the run went on -- in EITHER direction. BOTH are statements
   about this RUN and about the phone. Neither is a price for the governor, so THIS RUN PRINTS NO
   PRICE: there is no control window to compare against, and window C is not read at all.

   What it does establish is the phone's own thermal behaviour under this intervention, and that is
   worth having: it is the number a longer wait would have to beat. If you want the price anyway,
   --settle-back 0 runs the old design and --margin N loosens the bar; both are printed in the
   verdict so nobody has to guess which one was used.

== no window C -- there is no control window in this run, so there is no verdict to print
