--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
C. THE TIMELINE, from /proc/uptime (the device clock is wrong; uptime is not)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  uptime    what                                  busy
  19861     pre.1   (first pre-hold sample)       1.04 of 4 cores
  19895     pre.2   (second: this is the plateau) 1.05
  19919     window A starts                      0.82
  19997     window B starts (cores PINNED)       0.54
  ~20062    the undo is done, and the wait starts (B's 45 s + the 20 s settle)
  20860     the last sample of the wait -- and the phone is 4.1 C BELOW window A on thermal_zone9

  THE WAIT COST 798 SECONDS OF WALL CLOCK AGAINST A 240 SECOND BOUND -- 24 samples, i.e. about 33 s each,
  and each sample is `zl1-thermal.sh --seconds 10`: its own 10 s window PLUS the time this device takes to
  walk /proc for its per-process snapshot. So the bound counts the SLEEPS, and the samples are charged on
  top of it -- on this phone, about 3.3 times the bound. The run's own text says "within 240s", which is
  the bound and not the wall clock, and that is a defect this run found (docs 173 section 5).

  The whole run spanned uptime 19861 -> ~20870: 1009 s, about 17 MINUTES, of which the wait was 13.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
D. WHAT WAS AND WAS NOT ESTABLISHED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  ESTABLISHED:  (1) the pre-hold works on the real phone -- the phone was HOLDING STILL (the largest change
                    on any tsens zone in 10 s was 0.4 C) before window A was read, and it said so;
                (2) the phone is a phone whose own state moves more than this experiment can resolve inside
                    one run: 4.1 C of difference from window A, in the OPPOSITE direction to the
                    intervention, measured AFTER the cores were put back;
                (3) the instrument refuses a price for it, does not read window C, restores the cores, and
                    prints the direction of the difference that stopped it.

  NOT ESTABLISHED, and this is the whole point: WHAT THE SECOND HEAT CAUSE IS WORTH IN TEMPERATURE. Three
  device runs have now been made with this instrument and NONE of them has produced a price -- one
  CONTAMINATED (docs 170), one that printed an artifact (docs 172), and this one, which refused.
